{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.5,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["REG1", "C4"],
      "pins": ["REG1.17", "REG1.18", "REG1.19", "REG1.27", "REG1.28", "REG1.30"],
      "nets": ["REG_VIN", "VCC"],
      "observed": "On the Power sheet, the MIC26903YJL-TR (REG1) input-side pins are split across two nets: pins 13-16 (PVIN_1-4) are tied to the battery-input net VCC (which also feeds C1, C2 and R2), while pins 17-19 (PVIN_5-7), 27 (VIN), 28 (VDD) and 30 (PVIN_8) plus decoupling cap C4 are tied to a separate net labelled REG_VIN. No wire or global label joins REG_VIN to VCC anywhere on the root Power sheet (REG_VIN appears only on this sheet), so the controller's VIN/VDD and four of its eight PVIN pins are left floating.",
      "expected": "The specification states the buck 'steps the battery input down to 5 V' from the XT60/VCC rail; all PVIN pins and the VIN pin of a synchronous buck must connect to the same battery input net (VCC) for the regulator to be powered and to start.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet, REG1 MIC26903YJL-TR: '13 PVIN_1, 14 PVIN_2, 15 PVIN_3, 16 PVIN_4' wired to the VCC arrow (with C1/C2/R2); '17 PVIN_5, 18 PVIN_6, 19 PVIN_7, 27 VIN, 28 VDD, 30 PVIN_8' wired to the box net REG_VIN (with C4). No REG_VIN-to-VCC connection is drawn."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "A MIC26903YJJ-TR synchronous buck regulator steps the battery input down to 5 V."}
      ],
      "impact": "The buck controller's supply/sense pins (VIN, VDD, PVIN_5-8) receive no input power, so the regulator cannot start. The 5 V rail (and therefore the 3.3 V LDO and the entire board) would be dead, or the part could be latched up / behave unpredictably.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing otherwise matches the specification (R1=10 mΩ, L1=15 µH, R5/R6 giving ~5 V, correct I2C addresses, correct LDO/buck parts, the documented barometer pin-swap bug is not an anomaly), but the MIC26903 buck's VIN/VDD/PVIN_5-8 pins sit on a REG_VIN net that is never connected to the battery input VCC, leaving the regulator's power input floating - a deviation from the intended power stage."
}

